      "side": 1,
      "message": "Thanks for your professional reply. I understand your concern about the core problem of WeakGlobalReference process caused by FinalizerReferences. Our design is close to some ideas of ART.\n\nThere are two kinds of weak references in ART, WeakReference stored in the heap and GlobalWeakReference stored in WeakGlobalTable. We deal with GlobalWeakReference that blocks application rendering.\n\nThe DisableWeakReferenceAccess phase mainly consists of two parts. The first part, ProcessReferences(), includes the processing of WeakReference; the second part, SweepSystemWeaks(), includes the processing of GlobalWeakReference.\n\nAccording to the ART code, the ProcessReferences() function handles WeakReference in two stages.\n1. Before processing FinalizerReference, directly clean up the referent according to the result of the mark stage\n2. After processing FinalizerReference and clearing MarkStack, process WeakReference that can only be reached from FinalizerReference\n\nHowever, in ART code, the processing of GlobalWeakReference is after the processing of FinalizerReference. In addition, we claim that the processing of GlobalWeakReference should refer to stage 1 in ProcessReferences(), since there should be no GlobalWeakReference that can only be reached from FinalizerReference.\n\nAccording to the results of our profiling of applications such as Douyin, the main cost of ProcessReferences() comes from processing FinalizerReferences, and the main cost of SweepSystemWeaks() comes from SweepRootTables. If our claim is correct, these parts with high overhead should not affect the results of GlobalWeakReference.Get(). Hence, our design is theoretically possible.\n\nIf there are still problems with our design, please answer again.",
